00:02:49.490 --> 00:02:53.090
If that sounds good, let's
start with something a customer

00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:55.280
told me last year.

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:58.130
So as you're reading
this, remember

00:02:58.130 --> 00:03:01.250
this is a company that the vast
majority of people in this room

00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:03.385
probably use, or at
least have heard of.

00:03:03.385 --> 00:03:05.990
I've redacted their name
as getting legal approval

00:03:05.990 --> 00:03:09.950
in time for next
consult on [INAUDIBLE]..

00:03:09.950 --> 00:03:13.630
So to put this in context, this
company is forward leaning.

00:03:13.630 --> 00:03:17.570
They have infrastructure
on prem, on GKE on GCP.

00:03:17.570 --> 00:03:19.310
But given that
GKE is declarative

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:21.530
and GCP is imperative,
they run into the problem

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:24.780
of fragmented management
language matrix.

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:27.020
So how did we end up all here?

00:03:27.020 --> 00:03:29.270
To understand that, let's
talk about the first secular

00:03:29.270 --> 00:03:31.850
trend that has taken
the world by storm--

00:03:31.850 --> 00:03:34.820
the adoption of
infrastructure as code.

00:03:34.820 --> 00:03:36.320
It seems like a lifetime away.

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:39.080
But only a few years back,
many of us in this room

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:41.530
were still doing
configuration by hand.

00:03:41.530 --> 00:03:43.340
How well has public
cloud provider

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:44.880
have achieved
multiple 9 reliability

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:46.130
on our infrastructure.

00:03:46.130 --> 00:03:48.080
Increasingly errors
are not caused

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:50.420
because of hardware problems,
but because somebody's fat

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:53.540
fingered an extra zero,
pressed the wrong button.

00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:57.320
For the most part, we can all
agree that manual configuration

00:03:57.320 --> 00:03:59.273
is mostly behind us.

00:03:59.273 --> 00:04:01.190
And the advantage of
putting everything in Git

00:04:01.190 --> 00:04:02.095
is tremendous.

00:04:02.095 --> 00:04:03.470
Once you have
everything in Git--

00:04:03.470 --> 00:04:05.780
and you can start applying
basically the last 30

00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:07.610
years of software
management best

00:04:07.610 --> 00:04:09.522
practices to these resources.

00:04:09.522 --> 00:04:11.230
And you can start
treating infrastructure

00:04:11.230 --> 00:04:14.150
as zeros and ones and
leveraging Git workforce to do

00:04:14.150 --> 00:04:16.079
some very interesting things.

00:04:16.079 --> 00:04:19.670
Let me give you three examples
of what I've seen customers do.

00:04:19.670 --> 00:04:22.460
The first example
is approval flaws

00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:23.880
in highly regulated environment.

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:25.730
So if you're working
at a startup,

00:04:25.730 --> 00:04:29.000
you may laugh at this, that some
companies have to get approval

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:31.730
from their VP or
directors of IT before you

00:04:31.730 --> 00:04:34.100
can approve spending
on infrastructure

00:04:34.100 --> 00:04:34.940
at a certain level.

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:39.410
How would finance, insurance,
government, health care,

00:04:39.410 --> 00:04:42.190
being able to leverage Git
workforce for approvals

00:04:42.190 --> 00:04:44.150
is not only a
nice-to-have feature,

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:46.830
but it's a regulatory
requirement.

00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:49.380
Another great example is the
notion of pre submit check.

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:53.120
GCP recently erased a perk
called config validator--

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:54.200
it's an EAP--

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:57.620
that allows you to ensure that
your policies and structure

00:04:57.620 --> 00:05:00.220
articulated in code
are not in violation

00:05:00.220 --> 00:05:02.570
with their overall
automation policies.

00:05:02.570 --> 00:05:04.790
So imagine a scenario
where you spin up

00:05:04.790 --> 00:05:08.240
a Cloud Function for a few
seconds, config validator--

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:10.940
ensure that the function is
compliant, saving you hours

00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:13.640
of rework or even worse later.

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:16.330
And my last example is
my favorite, code reds.

00:05:16.330 --> 00:05:19.370
Not that I like code reds,
but it's a good example.

00:05:19.370 --> 00:05:21.640
I met a customer
last year in New York

00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:24.320
who has a pre agreed
template on who gets access

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:28.620
to water resources in case
of a code red scenario,

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:30.200
if something bad really happens.

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:32.480
And she is able to give
hundreds of engineers

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:36.200
access to GCP environments in
case something bad happens.

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:38.480
Additionally, because this
is now part of a code drive

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:40.140
that her leadership
has agreed upon,

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:43.580
she is able to simulate a
coded drill, giving developers

00:05:43.580 --> 00:05:46.530
the access they need to fix
the issues that they are seeing

00:05:46.530 --> 00:05:48.870
and to roll back the
access once a fix has

00:05:48.870 --> 00:05:50.110
been formed and deployed.

00:05:50.110 --> 00:05:53.730
And the best part of this story
is that you don't have to be FC

00:05:53.730 --> 00:05:57.000
or snap to take advantage
of these dev ops practices.

00:05:57.000 --> 00:05:59.700
Fortune 500 companies,
government agencies

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:02.370
are adopting these
workflows so that they

00:06:02.370 --> 00:06:04.980
can recover from failure
faster, deploy more often,

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:06.460
and deploy more quickly.

00:06:06.460 --> 00:06:09.060
And all of this has
been really exciting.

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:12.090
As in the last few
years, many startups

00:06:12.090 --> 00:06:14.370
and established players
have entered into the fray

00:06:14.370 --> 00:06:16.620
and trying to simplify
how you manage and deploy

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:17.730
infrastructure.

00:06:17.730 --> 00:06:20.700
As I see this, this kind of
happening in three waves.

00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:23.340
I started with CI/CD for
code, which is now pretty well

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:26.950
established and you have a
lot of options to choose from.

00:06:26.950 --> 00:06:30.060
Then configured code came in
as part of the second wave

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:33.110
with Google's Cloud Deployment
Manager, CHEF, Pulumi,

00:06:33.110 --> 00:06:35.190
Terraform leading the way.

00:06:35.190 --> 00:06:37.470
And everything for code
is starting to take off,

00:06:37.470 --> 00:06:40.290
but customers wanted to put
their own policies, router

00:06:40.290 --> 00:06:44.340
configurations, firewall rules,
VPCs, everything into Git.

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:46.890
And with all these
different tools and services

00:06:46.890 --> 00:06:49.320
in the space, customers
have a broad set

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:52.300
of options and standards and
languages to choose from.

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:53.940
And often, these
options and standards

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:55.315
do not work well
with each other.

00:06:55.315 --> 00:06:58.613
But we'll talk about
that a bit later.

00:06:58.613 --> 00:07:00.030
For now, let's
shift gear and talk

00:07:00.030 --> 00:07:03.720
about the next secular trend
that I have been observing--

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:05.760
the rise and adoption
of Kubernetes.

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:08.520
For many of you in this
room, this is not news.

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:10.560
However, given the
diversity of the audience--

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:13.020
and let me quickly talk to
some publicly available data

00:07:13.020 --> 00:07:15.788
and what is driving the
adoption of Kubernetes.

00:07:15.788 --> 00:07:17.830
Over the first few years,
we have seen Kubernetes

00:07:17.830 --> 00:07:21.420
steadily gaining in
usage on every platform.

00:07:21.420 --> 00:07:23.880
But nowhere has it been
more popular than on GCP.

00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:26.700
As most of you are aware,
Kubernetes was originally

00:07:26.700 --> 00:07:27.990
developed at Google.

00:07:27.990 --> 00:07:30.300
And we continue to lead the
innovation in the space.

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:32.030
According to a
survey by Datadog,

00:07:32.030 --> 00:07:35.880
organizations running
containers in GCP, more than 85%

00:07:35.880 --> 00:07:38.460
orchestrate workloads
with Kubernetes.

00:07:38.460 --> 00:07:41.760
In Azure, which has
offered a manage Kubernetes

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:44.790
service for about two
years now, roughly 65%

00:07:44.790 --> 00:07:47.220
of organizations
in using containers

00:07:47.220 --> 00:07:48.750
are also using Kubernetes.

00:07:48.750 --> 00:07:50.490
Kubernetes is
neutrally everywhere.

00:07:50.490 --> 00:07:52.470
Our customers run their
own Kubernetes clusters

00:07:52.470 --> 00:07:54.300
and diverse infrastructure
environments.

00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:55.920
And all three major
cloud providers

00:07:55.920 --> 00:07:58.770
offer to manage Kubernetes
service as well.

00:07:58.770 --> 00:08:04.280
So what is driving this adoption
and just to get into that like,

00:08:04.280 --> 00:08:08.700
hey, what is driving this,
think back in the day

00:08:08.700 --> 00:08:10.890
to when you're to
update software

00:08:10.890 --> 00:08:13.450
or when you're
pushing a deployment.

00:08:13.450 --> 00:08:15.070
There's usually a
lot of downtime.

00:08:15.070 --> 00:08:17.280
And I remember doing my
organizational wide upgrades

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:18.810
at midnight or over
the weekend when

00:08:18.810 --> 00:08:20.920
user traffic was at a minimum.

00:08:20.920 --> 00:08:22.980
Continuously deploying
new features with downtime

00:08:22.980 --> 00:08:24.618
does not increase velocity.

00:08:24.618 --> 00:08:26.160
The goal is to update
the application

00:08:26.160 --> 00:08:30.450
without any downtime, as your
users expect a constant uptime.

00:08:30.450 --> 00:08:33.030
Therefore, velocity is made
through a number of features

00:08:33.030 --> 00:08:35.460
you could ship per hour
while still maintaining

00:08:35.460 --> 00:08:37.559
a highly of level service.

00:08:37.559 --> 00:08:40.230
The core concepts of Kubernetes
which enable high velocity

00:08:40.230 --> 00:08:42.840
at immutability
decorative configuration

00:08:42.840 --> 00:08:44.120
and self-feeling system.

00:08:44.120 --> 00:08:45.870
So as far as the
developers are concerned,

00:08:45.870 --> 00:08:49.650
they apply the configuration in
Kubernetes using QCUtil apply.

00:08:49.650 --> 00:08:51.810
And they can focus on
higher level functions

00:08:51.810 --> 00:08:53.838
and not worry about downtime.

00:08:53.838 --> 00:08:55.380
Also as your products
grows, you have

00:08:55.380 --> 00:08:58.020
to scale both your software
and use working on it.

00:08:58.020 --> 00:08:59.610
So Kubernetes
achieve scalability

00:08:59.610 --> 00:09:02.920
by favoring decouple
microservice architectures.

00:09:02.920 --> 00:09:05.040
In a decoupled
architecture, each component

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:06.960
is separated from
other components

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:10.350
by defined APIs and
service load balances.

00:09:10.350 --> 00:09:13.410
APIs provides a buffer between
implementer and consumer

00:09:13.410 --> 00:09:15.630
and/or balances provide
a buffer between running

00:09:15.630 --> 00:09:18.060
instances of a service.

00:09:18.060 --> 00:09:21.510
Decoupling components while
lower balances makes it easier

00:09:21.510 --> 00:09:23.358
to scale the
program that make up

00:09:23.358 --> 00:09:25.650
your service because increasing
the size of the program

00:09:25.650 --> 00:09:28.230
can be done without
adjusting or reconfiguring

00:09:28.230 --> 00:09:30.823
any of the other
layers of your service.

00:09:30.823 --> 00:09:32.490
Let's talk about
suppression of concern.

00:09:32.490 --> 00:09:35.220
This topic should resonate
really well with you.

00:09:35.220 --> 00:09:38.243
When Google builds a service,
as a consumer of their service,

00:09:38.243 --> 00:09:39.660
you don't really
care about how we

00:09:39.660 --> 00:09:41.580
go about building that service.

00:09:41.580 --> 00:09:43.760
Similarly, in Kubernetes,
the application developer

00:09:43.760 --> 00:09:47.700
relies on the SLA delivered by
the continual orchestration API

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:49.710
without worrying about
how it is achieved.

00:09:49.710 --> 00:09:52.710
And likewise, the orchestration
API reliability engineer

00:09:52.710 --> 00:09:55.775
focuses on delivering
the orchestration API SLA

00:09:55.775 --> 00:09:57.900
without having to worry
about the applications that

00:09:57.900 --> 00:09:59.400
run on top of it.

00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:02.010
This separation, or
decoupling, means

00:10:02.010 --> 00:10:04.470
that a small team running
a Kubernetes cluster

00:10:04.470 --> 00:10:06.420
can be responsible for
supporting thousands

00:10:06.420 --> 00:10:08.320
of teams running on
their application

00:10:08.320 --> 00:10:09.780
within that cluster.

00:10:09.780 --> 00:10:11.850
And finally, because in
Kubernetes applications

00:10:11.850 --> 00:10:13.920
can be co-located
on the same machine

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:15.660
without impacting
each other, this

00:10:15.660 --> 00:10:17.160
means that tasks
from multiple users

00:10:17.160 --> 00:10:20.130
can be packed tightly
onto fewer machines.

00:10:20.130 --> 00:10:22.530
This in turns provides
a greater efficiency

00:10:22.530 --> 00:10:25.620
and also reduces the
cost of hardware,

00:10:25.620 --> 00:10:29.280
as less machines are used, which
means better cost efficiencies.

00:10:29.280 --> 00:10:30.840
So now that we are
on the same page,

00:10:30.840 --> 00:10:33.390
let's talk about a managed
configuration before Kubernetes

00:10:33.390 --> 00:10:34.870
arrived on the scene.

00:10:34.870 --> 00:10:37.390
Most organizations picked
an orchestrator of choice.

00:10:37.390 --> 00:10:40.260
So they could have
Jenkins or a Git of choice

00:10:40.260 --> 00:10:41.910
that they worked out of.

00:10:41.910 --> 00:10:44.870
Depending upon which
customer I am talking to,

00:10:44.870 --> 00:10:46.620
they will often have
separate organization

00:10:46.620 --> 00:10:49.288
managing their policies
versus their infrastructure.

00:10:49.288 --> 00:10:51.580
It's not necessary that,
within the same organization--

00:10:51.580 --> 00:10:52.760
especially if it's large--

00:10:52.760 --> 00:10:54.810
they agree on the
same choice of tools

00:10:54.810 --> 00:10:56.430
on both sides of the house.

00:10:56.430 --> 00:10:58.140
They will then couple
their orchestrator

00:10:58.140 --> 00:11:01.290
with one or more client
that does the actuation.

00:11:01.290 --> 00:11:04.470
And they may end up using Cloud
Deployment Manager, Pulumi,

00:11:04.470 --> 00:11:06.840
or Terraform for
their GCP resources

00:11:06.840 --> 00:11:08.490
and help for Kubernetes.

00:11:08.490 --> 00:11:10.200
This stacking and
mixing of tools

00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:13.980
has led to this fragmented
management and language matrix

00:11:13.980 --> 00:11:16.350
that I talked about where
developers and operators have

00:11:16.350 --> 00:11:18.900
to use a Rube
Goldbergish set of tools

00:11:18.900 --> 00:11:22.450
to manage different resources
type with different providers.

00:11:22.450 --> 00:11:25.350
The challenges is that any
two organizations today

00:11:25.350 --> 00:11:27.900
are not using these
tools in a similar way.

00:11:27.900 --> 00:11:32.300
And often they're confusing
inconsistencies across UIs,

00:11:32.300 --> 00:11:34.770
developing an
environment, command lines

00:11:34.770 --> 00:11:37.950
as decays configuration
tools and docks,

00:11:37.950 --> 00:11:41.460
leading to increased opportunity
for customers to make an error.

00:11:41.460 --> 00:11:43.140
As you can imagine,
this can often

00:11:43.140 --> 00:11:45.960
lead to workflows that
can be hard to orchestrate

00:11:45.960 --> 00:11:50.050
across non interoperable tools
that speak different languages.

00:11:50.050 --> 00:11:52.175
But wait, there is more.

00:11:52.175 --> 00:11:54.360
As Kubernetes
gaining wide option,

00:11:54.360 --> 00:11:57.720
increasingly customers have
to operate in the weird split

00:11:57.720 --> 00:11:58.800
brain mode.

00:11:58.800 --> 00:12:00.970
When you're managing
your Kubernetes cluster,

00:12:00.970 --> 00:12:02.640
you have this clean
decorative language

00:12:02.640 --> 00:12:04.050
where you order what you want.

00:12:04.050 --> 00:12:05.280
It's like ordering pizza.

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:07.980
You say medium
pepperoni, extra cheese,

00:12:07.980 --> 00:12:12.240
and the pizza shows up magically
in your doorstep in 30 minutes.

00:12:12.240 --> 00:12:14.700
However, when you're
managing your legacy cloud,

00:12:14.700 --> 00:12:17.870
you have to use imperative apps
with conflicting standards.

00:12:17.870 --> 00:12:20.690
Now you have to be precise and
described the entire recipe

00:12:20.690 --> 00:12:21.870
step by step.

00:12:21.870 --> 00:12:23.840
So if all you wanted
was a database

00:12:23.840 --> 00:12:26.853
on top of a sequel
running low on a VM,

00:12:26.853 --> 00:12:28.520
you have to go step
by step and you have

00:12:28.520 --> 00:12:30.800
to say, hey, spin up the VM.

00:12:30.800 --> 00:12:32.150
Then, you pull the thing.

00:12:32.150 --> 00:12:35.090
You wait for a couple of
minutes for the VM to come up.

00:12:35.090 --> 00:12:37.667
Then you're like, oh,
populate a sequel database.

00:12:37.667 --> 00:12:39.250
And then the sequel
database comes up.

00:12:39.250 --> 00:12:40.667
You pull it, and
then you're like,

00:12:40.667 --> 00:12:42.980
oh, you put the
database inside it.

00:12:42.980 --> 00:12:45.590
And you have to basically
ensure that every step was

00:12:45.590 --> 00:12:48.380
completed successfully before
you can do the next step.

00:12:48.380 --> 00:12:51.200
This creates cognitive
dissonance for our customers,

00:12:51.200 --> 00:12:54.440
and we decide to step in
and solve the problem.

00:12:54.440 --> 00:12:56.420
Config connector is
a Kubernetes plugin

00:12:56.420 --> 00:12:58.010
that allows the
resources implemented

00:12:58.010 --> 00:13:01.280
by non-Kubernetes APIs to be
managed declaratively using

00:13:01.280 --> 00:13:04.010
Kubernetes native
configuration and tooling.

00:13:04.010 --> 00:13:08.090
Users express their
declarative intent

00:13:08.090 --> 00:13:11.300
for config connector resources
as a [INAUDIBLE] YAML

00:13:11.300 --> 00:13:14.310
configuration, which can then
be applied to a GKE cluster,

00:13:14.310 --> 00:13:16.620
just like any other
Kubernetes source.

00:13:16.620 --> 00:13:18.620
The config connector
processes that the declared

00:13:18.620 --> 00:13:21.320
intent to the state of the
underlying resource, typically

00:13:21.320 --> 00:13:24.180
non declared and defined and
managed, and materializes it

00:13:24.180 --> 00:13:26.450
through calls to
the underlying API.

00:13:26.450 --> 00:13:29.630
Config connector also manages
the resources over time

00:13:29.630 --> 00:13:32.210
by continuously deconsign
the actual state

00:13:32.210 --> 00:13:34.400
of the underlying resource
with declarative intent.

00:13:34.400 --> 00:13:35.990
So what does that mean to you?

00:13:35.990 --> 00:13:37.760
So there are three
major advantages.

00:13:37.760 --> 00:13:39.860
Firstly, conflict
actor will make

00:13:39.860 --> 00:13:44.275
all GCP resources behave as if
they were Kubernetes resources.

00:13:44.275 --> 00:13:45.650
So now your
developers don't have

00:13:45.650 --> 00:13:47.628
to learn multiple
competing languages

00:13:47.628 --> 00:13:48.920
to manage their infrastructure.

00:13:48.920 --> 00:13:52.200
It's just a single
config.YAML file

00:13:52.200 --> 00:13:54.110
and containing all
infrastructure as code.

00:13:54.110 --> 00:13:57.710
And you QCUtil apply by the
single config YAML file.

00:13:57.710 --> 00:13:59.810
Secondly, you achieve
separation of concerns

00:13:59.810 --> 00:14:01.550
with eventual consistency.

00:14:01.550 --> 00:14:04.280
As a customer of GCP
using config connector,

00:14:04.280 --> 00:14:06.260
you do not really have
to care about how Google

00:14:06.260 --> 00:14:07.520
achieves the desired state.

00:14:07.520 --> 00:14:10.420
You can ask config connector
to spin up a database

00:14:10.420 --> 00:14:11.420
and populate data in it.

00:14:11.420 --> 00:14:14.120
And for some reason, if we
try to populate the data first

00:14:14.120 --> 00:14:16.332
a non instant database,
config connector

00:14:16.332 --> 00:14:18.290
will gracefully back off,
wait for the database

00:14:18.290 --> 00:14:21.110
to spin up, and then populate
the database once things

00:14:21.110 --> 00:14:22.130
are up and ready.

00:14:22.130 --> 00:14:24.060
And finally, because
config connector

00:14:24.060 --> 00:14:26.720
is relying on Kubernetes server
as a source of [INAUDIBLE],,

00:14:26.720 --> 00:14:28.760
the infrastructure
spun up a self-feeling.

00:14:28.760 --> 00:14:30.620
So if a miscarrier
intern comes in

00:14:30.620 --> 00:14:33.220
and fat fingered the
spanner, config connector

00:14:33.220 --> 00:14:35.300
will notice the
drift and correct it.

00:14:35.300 --> 00:14:37.073
To showcase all
of this and more,

00:14:37.073 --> 00:14:39.740
I'm going to ask Greg to talk us
through the next set of slides.

00:14:45.328 --> 00:14:46.370
GREGG DONOVAN: Excellent.

00:14:46.370 --> 00:14:48.410
Thank you.

00:14:48.410 --> 00:14:52.370
So I am, as I said,
Greg Donovan from Etsy.

00:14:52.370 --> 00:14:53.960
And for the last
18 months or so,

00:14:53.960 --> 00:14:55.760
I've been working
on our migration

00:14:55.760 --> 00:14:59.480
to GKE as part of Etsy's
larger GCP migration from our

00:14:59.480 --> 00:15:03.410
on prem, co-located
bare metal centers.

00:15:03.410 --> 00:15:05.900
And I'm looking forward to
sharing some of our lessons

00:15:05.900 --> 00:15:08.240
migrating from bare
metal to GKE and how

00:15:08.240 --> 00:15:11.080
config connector fits in.

00:15:11.080 --> 00:15:12.640
So what is Etsy?

00:15:12.640 --> 00:15:16.532
Etsy is the global marketplace
for unique and creative goods.

00:15:16.532 --> 00:15:20.320
It's a large international and
rapidly growing marketplace.

00:15:20.320 --> 00:15:22.690
We're constantly trying
to improve the product

00:15:22.690 --> 00:15:24.920
to keep up with our
buyers and sellers' needs,

00:15:24.920 --> 00:15:28.100
making development
velocity paramount.

00:15:28.100 --> 00:15:29.960
Search at Etsy,
which I worked on,

00:15:29.960 --> 00:15:31.790
is an especially fun problem.

00:15:31.790 --> 00:15:33.650
It combines large scale
systems and machine

00:15:33.650 --> 00:15:36.110
learning problems,
real world impact,

00:15:36.110 --> 00:15:40.265
and lots of interesting
products to think about.

00:15:40.265 --> 00:15:42.640
Etsy's headquarters is in
Brooklyn, where it was founded.

00:15:42.640 --> 00:15:44.348
And we also have
engineering offices here

00:15:44.348 --> 00:15:47.050
in San Francisco, in
Toronto, Dublin, and plenty

00:15:47.050 --> 00:15:49.420
of remotes around the world.

00:15:49.420 --> 00:15:52.220
Etsy started in 2005,
before the cloud.

00:15:52.220 --> 00:15:56.200
And from 2005 to 2017, all
the company's search services

00:15:56.200 --> 00:15:59.600
ran in our co-located
data centers.

00:15:59.600 --> 00:16:02.380
Till 2016, everything was
pure bare metal servers--

00:16:02.380 --> 00:16:04.750
no VMs, no containers.

00:16:04.750 --> 00:16:08.980
Then in 2016, we built our
first on prem Kubernetes cluster

00:16:08.980 --> 00:16:11.620
and began migrating our
search applications over time

00:16:11.620 --> 00:16:12.850
to Kubernetes.

00:16:12.850 --> 00:16:17.050
In 2017, the company
decided to move to GCP.

00:16:17.050 --> 00:16:19.630
Our on prem Kubernetes
migration morphed

00:16:19.630 --> 00:16:24.420
into a GKE migration, which
we finished up this spring.

00:16:24.420 --> 00:16:26.470
When we started
our GCP migration,

00:16:26.470 --> 00:16:30.270
we decided to use Terraform to
provision our GCP resources.

00:16:30.270 --> 00:16:31.770
We used Terraform
for everything,

00:16:31.770 --> 00:16:34.020
except for our applications
and the Kubernetes

00:16:34.020 --> 00:16:36.100
objects that they create.

00:16:36.100 --> 00:16:37.290
So what is Terraform?

00:16:37.290 --> 00:16:39.540
For those unfamiliar,
Terraform is a framework

00:16:39.540 --> 00:16:41.310
for provisioning
cloud resources using

00:16:41.310 --> 00:16:44.460
a declarative configuration
language called HCL.

00:16:44.460 --> 00:16:47.370
In addition to GCP, it
supports the AWS, Azure,

00:16:47.370 --> 00:16:50.730
other infrastructure providers
via their plugin framework.

00:16:50.730 --> 00:16:53.640
Underneath Terraform
represents our resources

00:16:53.640 --> 00:16:55.620
as a graph with
their dependencies.

00:16:55.620 --> 00:16:57.210
When you make
Terraform changes, it

00:16:57.210 --> 00:16:59.820
needs to reconcile your declared
state with the actual state

00:16:59.820 --> 00:17:01.080
of these resources.

00:17:01.080 --> 00:17:04.710
For example, does that the
GKE cluster name search exist?

00:17:04.710 --> 00:17:06.843
Does it have the node
pools we've requested?

00:17:06.843 --> 00:17:08.760
And if there is the drift
between your desired

00:17:08.760 --> 00:17:10.343
state and the current
state, Terraform

00:17:10.343 --> 00:17:12.300
will figure out
which API calls it

00:17:12.300 --> 00:17:14.430
needs to make in
order to reconcile

00:17:14.430 --> 00:17:16.861
the desired and actual state.

00:17:16.861 --> 00:17:18.569
And if you're interested
in learning more

00:17:18.569 --> 00:17:20.140
about the underlying
graph at work,

00:17:20.140 --> 00:17:22.589
there's a great talk called
applying graph theory

00:17:22.589 --> 00:17:26.160
to infrastructure as code
that I've found very helpful.

00:17:26.160 --> 00:17:27.780
And to deploy
Terraform changes, we

00:17:27.780 --> 00:17:30.650
have a CI pipeline that
uses Jenkins, validates

00:17:30.650 --> 00:17:33.090
that a Terraform
change is valid,

00:17:33.090 --> 00:17:35.040
gives you the output
from a Terraform plan,

00:17:35.040 --> 00:17:37.800
and then, if a human approves
the plan, applies it.

00:17:37.800 --> 00:17:40.110
We have a single
Terraform monorepo

00:17:40.110 --> 00:17:43.140
with all the company's
cloud infrastructure giving

00:17:43.140 --> 00:17:46.140
a single place to audit,
refactor, and a single CI/CD

00:17:46.140 --> 00:17:48.600
pipeline for all of us.

00:17:48.600 --> 00:17:51.840
Now over in GKE,
where search is,

00:17:51.840 --> 00:17:53.640
Kubernetes does
something very similar.

00:17:53.640 --> 00:17:56.010
Our declared state is in
our YAML configuration.

00:17:56.010 --> 00:17:58.320
The actual state is
determined by Kubernetes,

00:17:58.320 --> 00:18:02.060
usually by consulting the Etsy
D database that backs its API.

00:18:02.060 --> 00:18:04.470
New call kubectl
apply, Kubernetes dates

00:18:04.470 --> 00:18:06.600
will begin to reconcile
what you expressed

00:18:06.600 --> 00:18:10.260
that you wanted in YAML with
what's actually in existence.

00:18:10.260 --> 00:18:13.080
So both Terraform and
Kubernetes have graphs

00:18:13.080 --> 00:18:14.760
with a notion of dependencies.

00:18:14.760 --> 00:18:17.430
Each graph knows how to
do a topological sort,

00:18:17.430 --> 00:18:20.610
figure out what the order
that it should apply changes.

00:18:20.610 --> 00:18:23.272
The Kubernetes API
is a wonderful thing.

00:18:23.272 --> 00:18:25.230
We're able to express
our infrastructure richly

00:18:25.230 --> 00:18:27.450
with an API that works
on any cloud provider

00:18:27.450 --> 00:18:28.950
on prem, et cetera.

00:18:28.950 --> 00:18:31.870
And we have assembled built
in Kubernetes objects,

00:18:31.870 --> 00:18:33.930
like deployment staples
sets, et cetera,

00:18:33.930 --> 00:18:36.540
that can model our
infrastructure declaratively.

00:18:36.540 --> 00:18:39.240
We also take advantage of the
Kubernetes extension mechanism,

00:18:39.240 --> 00:18:42.150
custom resource definitions,
to model objects

00:18:42.150 --> 00:18:43.680
at a higher level.

00:18:43.680 --> 00:18:45.540
For example, we use
the Prometheus Operator

00:18:45.540 --> 00:18:46.980
to deploy Prometheus.

00:18:46.980 --> 00:18:49.410
It uses a set of custom
resource definitions

00:18:49.410 --> 00:18:51.990
to model objects that
are the core abstractions

00:18:51.990 --> 00:18:53.640
in the Prometheus installation--

00:18:53.640 --> 00:18:55.920
Prometheus, AlertManager,
ServiceMonitors,

00:18:55.920 --> 00:18:57.690
and PrometheusRules.

00:18:57.690 --> 00:19:00.180
These then become top
level objects in Kubernetes

00:19:00.180 --> 00:19:02.340
that we can manipulate
with kubectl.

00:19:02.340 --> 00:19:05.100
So I can do kubectl
get service monitors

00:19:05.100 --> 00:19:07.110
and see a list of
directives for Prometheus

00:19:07.110 --> 00:19:09.270
about what to scrape.

00:19:09.270 --> 00:19:11.590
CRDs make a lot of sense
when you analogize them

00:19:11.590 --> 00:19:13.120
to programming languages.

00:19:13.120 --> 00:19:15.655
If pods, services,
config maps, et cetera

00:19:15.655 --> 00:19:18.430
are like your primitive types,
custom resource definitions

00:19:18.430 --> 00:19:20.530
allow you to build higher
level abstractions out

00:19:20.530 --> 00:19:21.800
of these primitives.

00:19:21.800 --> 00:19:24.660
They're like Java classes
but for Kubernetes.

00:19:24.660 --> 00:19:28.030
And Kubernetes has, to quote
Kelsey Hightower "become

00:19:28.030 --> 00:19:31.760
the POSIX API for
distributed systems."

00:19:31.760 --> 00:19:34.510
And so at Etsy, we deploy all
of our search services to GKE.

00:19:34.510 --> 00:19:37.660
We use the tech stack on this
slide for our Kubernetes CI/CD

00:19:37.660 --> 00:19:41.162
pipeline, which we'll see
more of in the upcoming demo.

00:19:41.162 --> 00:19:43.120
Suffice it to say, we've
invested a huge amount

00:19:43.120 --> 00:19:45.700
of time and effort to make this
pipeline as fast and powerful

00:19:45.700 --> 00:19:47.420
as we can.

00:19:47.420 --> 00:19:50.320
It offers speedy incremental
reproducible builds

00:19:50.320 --> 00:19:53.500
using Bazel, especially for
incremental docker container

00:19:53.500 --> 00:19:55.870
layer builds and
programmatic control

00:19:55.870 --> 00:20:00.280
using Python over the Kubernetes
YAML generated specifically

00:20:00.280 --> 00:20:01.830
for each environment.

00:20:01.830 --> 00:20:03.310
And our goal is
to make deploying

00:20:03.310 --> 00:20:06.430
to Kubernetes fast, reliable,
as customizable as you

00:20:06.430 --> 00:20:08.800
need to be, and as
simple as we can make it.

00:20:08.800 --> 00:20:10.690
We want our search
repo to be the one

00:20:10.690 --> 00:20:13.390
place you need to make changes
to launch a new feature

00:20:13.390 --> 00:20:16.580
or create a brand new testing
environment in, for example,

00:20:16.580 --> 00:20:19.130
a PR driven workflow.

00:20:19.130 --> 00:20:21.630
So if this seems intriguing,
I gave a talk at BazelCon

00:20:21.630 --> 00:20:23.423
last year about this stack.

00:20:23.423 --> 00:20:24.840
And I'll be speaking
more about it

00:20:24.840 --> 00:20:28.000
at CouCon in
Barcelona next month.

00:20:28.000 --> 00:20:30.660
Or just email me.

00:20:30.660 --> 00:20:35.190
Unfortunately, this stack
has a glaring weakness.

00:20:35.190 --> 00:20:38.100
Anytime you want to make
changes to GCP growth resources,

00:20:38.100 --> 00:20:39.260
it doesn't help us.

00:20:39.260 --> 00:20:41.820
Our two worlds,
Terraform and Kubernetes,

00:20:41.820 --> 00:20:43.950
do not interact
easily, adding lots

00:20:43.950 --> 00:20:46.740
of friction, fragility, and
complexity to the development

00:20:46.740 --> 00:20:47.880
process.

00:20:47.880 --> 00:20:51.010
Config connector is designed
to solve this problem.

00:20:51.010 --> 00:20:53.830
So let's show config connector
in action in a real setting.

00:20:53.830 --> 00:20:56.370
I'd like to show you
two examples at Etsy

00:20:56.370 --> 00:20:58.470
that we targeted for which
config connector could

00:20:58.470 --> 00:21:00.850
make our day to day
development easier.

00:21:00.850 --> 00:21:02.490
So as I noted,
search is organized

00:21:02.490 --> 00:21:06.090
into a large monorepo built
with the Bazel build tool.

00:21:06.090 --> 00:21:08.310
Since I can't open
source our entire code

00:21:08.310 --> 00:21:13.050
base for legal reasons, I
created a demo app on GitHub

00:21:13.050 --> 00:21:14.970
that serves as a
microcosm of our code

00:21:14.970 --> 00:21:17.820
base with a whittled
down version of much

00:21:17.820 --> 00:21:22.290
of the scaffolding that we use
in this Kubernetes delivery

00:21:22.290 --> 00:21:23.950
pipeline.

00:21:23.950 --> 00:21:26.903
So you can see it here on GitHub
if you want to pull it up.

00:21:26.903 --> 00:21:29.070
And the demo is going to
show off two issues that we

00:21:29.070 --> 00:21:30.710
targeted for config connector.

00:21:30.710 --> 00:21:34.170
The first is how to
debug a problematic JVM.

00:21:34.170 --> 00:21:37.500
So for JVM diagnostics, we
used the YourKit profiler

00:21:37.500 --> 00:21:41.550
with its detailed CPU memory
and event snapshot files.

00:21:41.550 --> 00:21:43.710
And on an application
exit, we are seeing them

00:21:43.710 --> 00:21:46.790
from a pod local
directory to a GCS bucket.

00:21:46.790 --> 00:21:48.480
And we wanted the
GCS bucket names

00:21:48.480 --> 00:21:51.330
to be dynamic based on their
environment, some combination

00:21:51.330 --> 00:21:53.640
of the GKE cluster,
the namespace,

00:21:53.640 --> 00:21:57.090
the pod name so the snapshots
were well organized,

00:21:57.090 --> 00:21:59.160
didn't step on
each other's toes.

00:21:59.160 --> 00:22:01.980
The second issue we wanted
config connector to help with

00:22:01.980 --> 00:22:03.360
is structured logging.

00:22:03.360 --> 00:22:06.240
So for certain use cases,
we'd like to stream

00:22:06.240 --> 00:22:09.240
logs directly to BigQuery so
that we can perform analytics

00:22:09.240 --> 00:22:10.410
on them.

00:22:10.410 --> 00:22:13.310
And again, we want a dynamic
one to one mapping of data

00:22:13.310 --> 00:22:17.110
set to application environment.

00:22:17.110 --> 00:22:19.150
Before config
connector, we would

00:22:19.150 --> 00:22:20.920
need to go to
Terraform every time

00:22:20.920 --> 00:22:22.580
we created a new environment.

00:22:22.580 --> 00:22:24.880
So if you imagine that PR
driven workflow where you've

00:22:24.880 --> 00:22:26.830
got some changes
you want to make,

00:22:26.830 --> 00:22:29.380
before actually going to search
and deploying your changes,

00:22:29.380 --> 00:22:30.910
you've got to go
over to Terraform.

00:22:30.910 --> 00:22:32.743
And you've got to follow
all of these steps.

00:22:32.743 --> 00:22:35.170
And there might be
some I've forgotten.

00:22:35.170 --> 00:22:37.600
And what makes it more
difficult is that, for example,

00:22:37.600 --> 00:22:39.310
for a GCS bucket
that you might want

00:22:39.310 --> 00:22:43.330
to create for your namespace,
we have random slugs attached

00:22:43.330 --> 00:22:46.670
to them for security reasons.

00:22:46.670 --> 00:22:49.850
And then Terraform state
files are encrypted.

00:22:49.850 --> 00:22:52.550
So it adds a lot of steps.

00:22:52.550 --> 00:22:54.550
And another problem is
Terraform's configuration

00:22:54.550 --> 00:22:55.090
language.

00:22:55.090 --> 00:22:56.590
It's very, very limited.

00:22:56.590 --> 00:22:59.140
It makes a complex real
world configuration

00:22:59.140 --> 00:23:01.880
like we have at
Etsy very difficult.

00:23:01.880 --> 00:23:05.050
For example, a simple
for loop over a map

00:23:05.050 --> 00:23:07.540
is almost impossible to
do without reading a blog

00:23:07.540 --> 00:23:09.438
post about it in Terraform.

00:23:12.035 --> 00:23:13.550
Ah, we're not up to that.

00:23:13.550 --> 00:23:14.770
So let's go to the demo.

00:23:23.300 --> 00:23:27.230
So this is the script
we're going to run.

00:23:27.230 --> 00:23:31.200
It's the main method of our
pod that starts up a server.

00:23:31.200 --> 00:23:33.350
And then when it
exits, it copies all

00:23:33.350 --> 00:23:38.210
of these snapshot files to GCS.

00:23:38.210 --> 00:23:41.166
And here is the service itself.

00:23:41.166 --> 00:23:43.045
It sets up BigQuery.

00:23:43.045 --> 00:23:49.660
It sets up some URL handlers,
writes to our structure logs,

00:23:49.660 --> 00:23:52.150
can say hello and goodbye,
just a simple application

00:23:52.150 --> 00:23:53.600
to show how this works.

00:23:53.600 --> 00:23:55.142
And then this is
the script that gets

00:23:55.142 --> 00:24:01.220
called on shutdown to write
those snapshots to the GSC.

00:24:01.220 --> 00:24:04.450
And we can also show the
structured log, which

00:24:04.450 --> 00:24:06.895
is writing rows to BigQuery.

00:24:11.650 --> 00:24:14.440
And here is how we
use Python and Bazel

00:24:14.440 --> 00:24:19.960
to generate dynamic YAML
that we sent to Kubernetes.

00:24:19.960 --> 00:24:24.100
So for example, this
target is called subtargets

00:24:24.100 --> 00:24:27.730
that generate a dynamic
namespace, a dynamic GCS

00:24:27.730 --> 00:24:30.505
bucket, a dynamic BigQuery
data set, and then

00:24:30.505 --> 00:24:32.544
our regular
Kubernetes deployment.

00:24:35.260 --> 00:24:37.730
Now this is not the
most dynamic example.

00:24:37.730 --> 00:24:39.515
But if you imagine
QA environments

00:24:39.515 --> 00:24:41.890
versus test environments,
different things you might want

00:24:41.890 --> 00:24:45.100
to be testing, So this is
just a Python with PyYAML,

00:24:45.100 --> 00:24:47.560
generating a standard
Kubernetes deployment

00:24:47.560 --> 00:24:49.240
based on your context--

00:24:49.240 --> 00:24:53.650
what's your namespace, what's
your cluster, et cetera.

00:24:53.650 --> 00:24:55.280
So this is the application.

00:24:55.280 --> 00:24:57.250
Here is the service
that it uses.

00:24:57.250 --> 00:24:59.050
But the more
interesting part comes

00:24:59.050 --> 00:25:02.360
when config connector comes in.

00:25:02.360 --> 00:25:04.640
So here is how we
create a GCS bucket.

00:25:04.640 --> 00:25:06.920
So there's no switching
over to Terraform.

00:25:06.920 --> 00:25:09.380
We're using a CRD--

00:25:09.380 --> 00:25:11.990
so the config connector
CRD right here.

00:25:11.990 --> 00:25:15.800
And now we get a new type inside
of our Kubernetes cluster.

00:25:15.800 --> 00:25:17.350
We have a type called
storage bucket,

00:25:17.350 --> 00:25:19.400
and that is provided
by config connector

00:25:19.400 --> 00:25:21.950
that Sonam announced today.

00:25:21.950 --> 00:25:25.760
And you can see we set up the
standard GCS bucket attributes,

00:25:25.760 --> 00:25:28.440
its lifecycle, when it gets
deleted, after how long,

00:25:28.440 --> 00:25:29.510
et cetera.

00:25:29.510 --> 00:25:31.880
And then we do the
same for BigQuery.

00:25:31.880 --> 00:25:35.120
So we dynamically create
a BigQuery data set.

00:25:35.120 --> 00:25:38.210
We follow the BigQuery
whoops for how

00:25:38.210 --> 00:25:40.940
you have to name your
data sets, set up

00:25:40.940 --> 00:25:43.610
the table expiration, a
friendly name, et cetera.

00:25:43.610 --> 00:25:46.070
And then we've got this new
kind within our data set,

00:25:46.070 --> 00:25:48.080
a BigQuiery data set.

00:25:48.080 --> 00:25:50.740
So we've got these
new super powers.

00:25:50.740 --> 00:25:55.340
And I'll show you
one last bit, which

00:25:55.340 --> 00:26:02.420
is how we generate the
underlying docker container.

00:26:02.420 --> 00:26:06.630
So it's a Bazel
container image target.

00:26:06.630 --> 00:26:11.180
It takes a base docker
container, adds some scripts,

00:26:11.180 --> 00:26:19.240
adds the YourKit files,
and outputs a docker image.

00:26:19.240 --> 00:26:21.820
So let's show it to work.

00:26:21.820 --> 00:26:25.140
So let's see.

00:26:25.140 --> 00:26:29.940
We're in the CNRM GCP
next 19 demo namespace.

00:26:29.940 --> 00:26:32.460
That's where we want to be.

00:26:32.460 --> 00:26:33.865
Let's see.

00:26:33.865 --> 00:26:36.740
And do we have anything running?

00:26:36.740 --> 00:26:37.240
We don't.

00:26:40.390 --> 00:26:42.130
So we're going to
hit that target

00:26:42.130 --> 00:26:43.710
that we showed
before that's going

00:26:43.710 --> 00:26:46.030
to deploy all of those things.

00:26:46.030 --> 00:26:48.210
So we're going to try to
deploy it in one command--

00:26:48.210 --> 00:26:52.520
the namespace, the
deployment, the service,

00:26:52.520 --> 00:26:56.360
and the BigQuery data set,
and the GCS bucket-- so again,

00:26:56.360 --> 00:26:58.790
something that will require
previously multiple trips

00:26:58.790 --> 00:27:01.100
over to Terraform to
generate these things,

00:27:01.100 --> 00:27:03.620
cut and pasted
into our code base.

00:27:03.620 --> 00:27:05.480
But now it's one command.

00:27:20.960 --> 00:27:21.470
Here goes.

00:27:24.440 --> 00:27:24.940
There we go.

00:27:28.500 --> 00:27:33.740
So we created the namespace.

00:27:33.740 --> 00:27:35.230
We configured the GCS bucket.

00:27:35.230 --> 00:27:39.970
Now we can see these
things in action.

00:27:39.970 --> 00:27:43.420
So now we do have a pod.

00:27:43.420 --> 00:27:45.170
And we can go say hi to it.

00:27:45.170 --> 00:27:49.190
So let's first see that
it's up and running.

00:27:54.170 --> 00:27:57.490
OK, listening on port 8,000.

00:27:57.490 --> 00:27:58.200
Let's go say hi.

00:28:04.660 --> 00:28:06.426
So we'll set up port forwarding.

00:28:10.370 --> 00:28:13.340
So now we have a port
forward from my laptop

00:28:13.340 --> 00:28:17.330
to the pod running
inside of GKE.

00:28:17.330 --> 00:28:19.100
And we can curl it to say hello.

00:28:22.820 --> 00:28:23.840
Excellent.

00:28:23.840 --> 00:28:26.825
And now we should
be able to see,

00:28:26.825 --> 00:28:28.700
did it actually create
those storage buckets.

00:28:32.890 --> 00:28:34.620
Well, it shouldn't
actually-- yeah,

00:28:34.620 --> 00:28:36.400
so there's the storage bucket.

00:28:36.400 --> 00:28:39.440
And we can see
that the underlying

00:28:39.440 --> 00:28:42.326
CRD has been created.

00:28:42.326 --> 00:28:43.880
So let's describe it.

00:28:48.620 --> 00:28:50.930
And you can see the control
loop in the background.

00:28:50.930 --> 00:28:52.070
It says it's up to date.

00:28:52.070 --> 00:28:55.263
So given the YAML
we have here where

00:28:55.263 --> 00:28:56.430
we described what we wanted.

00:28:56.430 --> 00:29:00.140
We wanted a storage bucket
with these parameters

00:29:00.140 --> 00:29:02.127
deleted after seven days.

00:29:02.127 --> 00:29:04.460
And there's a control loop
in the background making sure

00:29:04.460 --> 00:29:09.162
that those GCS resources matches
what we had in the YAML spec.

00:29:09.162 --> 00:29:11.620
And we could see the same thing
with the BigQuery data set.

00:29:32.370 --> 00:29:34.040
And there it is.

00:29:34.040 --> 00:29:38.310
BigQuery data set exists,
as we wanted it to.

00:29:38.310 --> 00:29:41.355
But if we go to
the console, now we

00:29:41.355 --> 00:29:43.230
can see these resources
right in the console.

00:29:50.110 --> 00:29:51.870
And there they are.

00:29:51.870 --> 00:29:53.340
So the storage bucket exists.

00:29:53.340 --> 00:29:55.650
We can go over to BigQuery.

00:29:55.650 --> 00:30:01.820
Really, we did just create a
new data set just with cube CTL.

00:30:01.820 --> 00:30:02.790
And that's there.

00:30:02.790 --> 00:30:04.740
And there's our logs table.

00:30:04.740 --> 00:30:07.300
And we should have
some data in it.

00:30:12.635 --> 00:30:13.670
And there we go.

00:30:13.670 --> 00:30:16.400
So, when we said hello, it
wrote the hello message.

00:30:16.400 --> 00:30:19.690
So it's working.

00:30:19.690 --> 00:30:21.800
And now, when we
shut it down, it

00:30:21.800 --> 00:30:25.510
should write those GCS snapshots
to the dynamically created

00:30:25.510 --> 00:30:27.666
bucket--

00:30:27.666 --> 00:30:31.100
a very dramatic goodbye.

00:30:31.100 --> 00:30:33.160
So let's see if that
actually worked.

00:30:33.160 --> 00:30:34.160
It's listed out.

00:30:40.300 --> 00:30:42.050
Look at that.

00:30:42.050 --> 00:30:44.080
So those are the
logs and snapshots.

00:30:44.080 --> 00:30:47.320
So when the JVM exited,
it wrote these snapshots

00:30:47.320 --> 00:30:48.892
to the GCS bucket.

00:30:48.892 --> 00:30:50.850
And so all of this that
would have required you

00:30:50.850 --> 00:30:52.450
know multiple
trips to Terraform,

00:30:52.450 --> 00:30:56.350
lots of configuration, now
it's one cube CTL command,

00:30:56.350 --> 00:30:57.750
which is very exciting.

00:31:01.010 --> 00:31:02.380
So the demo's worked so far.

00:31:02.380 --> 00:31:06.910
Should we push our luck and
try something a little crazier?

00:31:06.910 --> 00:31:09.080
Let's make a new
test environment.

00:31:09.080 --> 00:31:12.447
So what should we call
our new test environment?

00:31:16.190 --> 00:31:17.870
Let's call it Michael.

00:31:17.870 --> 00:31:27.100
So we have this single
source of truth configuration

00:31:27.100 --> 00:31:30.910
for all of our applications,
their namespaces, et cetera.

00:31:30.910 --> 00:31:32.920
So here is where we
defined previously

00:31:32.920 --> 00:31:36.250
the namespace that we
wanted to have in the app

00:31:36.250 --> 00:31:38.860
that we were deploying, our
example app, in which cluster

00:31:38.860 --> 00:31:40.280
we wanted it to have it.

00:31:40.280 --> 00:31:44.860
So now we're going to make a
new namespace that didn't exist.

00:31:44.860 --> 00:31:52.223
So this is a Bazel.bzl
file that is defining this.

00:31:52.223 --> 00:31:53.140
We'll call it Michael.

00:31:56.026 --> 00:31:57.950
I'll call it Michael test.

00:32:02.770 --> 00:32:05.050
So let's find the
dynamically created targets.

00:32:16.700 --> 00:32:18.160
So there's five targets.

00:32:18.160 --> 00:32:21.200
We have a new one for
each of those things

00:32:21.200 --> 00:32:33.192
that we deployed previously
to the other namespace.

00:32:33.192 --> 00:32:35.150
And we'll deploy them to
the Michael namespace.

00:32:39.022 --> 00:32:42.192
It's a real demo.

00:32:42.192 --> 00:32:43.150
We're pushing our luck.

00:32:46.490 --> 00:32:48.320
So now it's going
to generate YAML

00:32:48.320 --> 00:32:52.690
with the new context this time,
the Michael test namespace.

00:32:52.690 --> 00:32:58.200
And it's going to the new
namespace, the new GCS buckets,

00:32:58.200 --> 00:33:00.430
the new BigQuery data
set, the new service,

00:33:00.430 --> 00:33:01.920
the new deployment.

00:33:01.920 --> 00:33:05.820
And let's actually switch
over to that namespace.

00:33:10.020 --> 00:33:13.350
And the one thing
that's manual right now

00:33:13.350 --> 00:33:16.990
is getting the proper
credentials in there.

00:33:16.990 --> 00:33:20.860
That is something that, with
workload identity that's

00:33:20.860 --> 00:33:25.400
being announced shortly, this
manual step will go away.

00:33:25.400 --> 00:33:27.550
So we put the credentials
that our pods need.

00:33:30.805 --> 00:33:36.125
And now let's see
if we have pods.

00:33:36.125 --> 00:33:37.115
We do.

00:33:43.070 --> 00:33:47.020
And so if we go over
here again, now we

00:33:47.020 --> 00:33:51.940
should see a new BigQuery data
set dynamically created just

00:33:51.940 --> 00:33:53.680
for this namespace.

00:33:53.680 --> 00:33:55.303
Let's cross our fingers.

00:33:55.303 --> 00:33:55.970
And there it is.

00:33:55.970 --> 00:33:58.300
There's the Michael
test data set.

00:33:58.300 --> 00:34:03.660
And that's up and going, and
we should see the GCS buckets.

00:34:07.542 --> 00:34:08.250
And there we are.

00:34:11.300 --> 00:34:13.929
So a dynamically
created test environment

00:34:13.929 --> 00:34:15.650
with a single command--

00:34:15.650 --> 00:34:18.610
so imagine this in the context
of a PR driven workflow.

00:34:18.610 --> 00:34:21.409
You have an engineer on the
team who wants testing changes.

00:34:21.409 --> 00:34:25.120
This is the fastest path to
getting a new test environment

00:34:25.120 --> 00:34:27.520
minted out with
your parameters that

00:34:27.520 --> 00:34:30.850
involve Kubernetes
infrastructure and GCP

00:34:30.850 --> 00:34:31.840
infrastructure.

00:34:31.840 --> 00:34:34.560
You can now do it in a single
command, what would previously

00:34:34.560 --> 00:34:38.864
have taken two CI/CD pipelines,
different approval steps,

00:34:38.864 --> 00:34:40.389
et cetera, et cetera--

00:34:40.389 --> 00:34:42.760
can now be done
just with cube CTL.

00:34:42.760 --> 00:34:47.630
So that is pretty
exciting for us.

00:34:47.630 --> 00:34:48.380
Now back to Sonam.

00:34:54.188 --> 00:34:55.480
SONAM SAXENA: That was amazing.

00:34:55.480 --> 00:34:56.147
Thank you, Greg.

00:34:56.147 --> 00:34:59.770
Can we switch back
to the-- oh, perfect.

00:34:59.770 --> 00:35:00.270
Magic.

00:35:03.990 --> 00:35:05.715
So that was amazing.

00:35:05.715 --> 00:35:07.090
Let's move on to
the Q&amp;A section.

00:35:07.090 --> 00:35:09.100
I'll ask Michael
to come up on stage

00:35:09.100 --> 00:35:10.950
as we get set up over here.

